1
00:02:03,056 --> 00:02:04,649
Thanks.

2
00:02:06,159 --> 00:02:07,457
Oh.

3
00:02:08,095 --> 00:02:09,119
We're gonna be late.

4
00:02:09,296 --> 00:02:10,457
For what?

5
00:02:10,631 --> 00:02:12,156
Law Club.

6
00:02:12,332 --> 00:02:16,963
Who cares? We should never
have joined that thing in the first place.

7
00:02:19,039 --> 00:02:21,008
It takes too much time.

8
00:02:28,849 --> 00:02:31,341
Didn't you ever get that
urge to just drop out of school?

9
00:02:31,518 --> 00:02:35,546
No. And neither should you. I
mean, you're top of the class.

10
00:02:35,722 --> 00:02:37,987
I break my back
and I barely get by.

11
00:02:38,458 --> 00:02:42,554
Huh. Well, maybe
I could help you.

12
00:02:42,729 --> 00:02:45,221
- Really?
- Sure.

13
00:02:45,832 --> 00:02:49,166
How would you like to
see a good art film tonight?

14
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:51,204
I don't know.

15
00:02:51,905 --> 00:02:54,841
About going with me
or seeing the movie?

16
00:02:56,009 --> 00:02:57,477
Well, I love good art.

17
00:02:57,644 --> 00:02:59,204
Don't answer now.
Look, I tell you what,

18
00:02:59,346 --> 00:03:00,939
I'll take you to
lunch after Law Club.

19
00:03:01,114 --> 00:03:03,015
Anywhere you want and
we'll talk about it then.

20
00:03:03,183 --> 00:03:05,152
Anywhere I want to go?

21
00:03:05,318 --> 00:03:07,184
Yeah. Yeah. Anywhere.

22
00:03:07,354 --> 00:03:09,289
I mean, practically. I
need tuition money.

23
00:03:09,456 --> 00:03:11,118
Okay.

24
00:03:12,859 --> 00:03:16,853
I was cross-examining
this widow one time

25
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:20,194
and I was pretty sure she
had killed her husband.

26
00:03:20,367 --> 00:03:23,428
She was a little, tiny woman. She
kept crying into her handkerchief

27
00:03:23,603 --> 00:03:28,234
and I was afraid if I went straight
at her, the jury might get mad at me.

28
00:03:28,408 --> 00:03:30,343
So I stayed on the slow side

29
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:34,709
to keep the jury on my side and I
was able to show reasonable doubt.

30
00:03:34,881 --> 00:03:37,282
And that's all a defense
attorney has to do. Yeah, Mark?

31
00:03:37,517 --> 00:03:40,112
Have you ever seen
the perfect murder?

32
00:03:40,287 --> 00:03:43,780
Well, I've seen cases
that were never solved.

33
00:03:43,957 --> 00:03:47,724
Yeah, but people do get away with it.
- Sometimes.

34
00:03:48,195 --> 00:03:49,458
It happens a lot.

35
00:03:49,629 --> 00:03:51,689
Heh, heh. More than we need.

36
00:03:51,865 --> 00:03:53,026
How do they pull it off?

37
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:56,637
- What do they do?
- Well, sometimes they get lucky.

38
00:03:57,637 --> 00:04:00,664
But with the
advances in forensics,

39
00:04:00,841 --> 00:04:05,142
the body alone can provide
enough evidence to identify the killer.

40
00:04:05,312 --> 00:04:07,747
So for a person to
commit the perfect murder,

41
00:04:07,914 --> 00:04:10,440
the first thing they'd have to
do is get rid of the body, right?

42
00:04:10,617 --> 00:04:13,178
Well, I'd say

43
00:04:13,353 --> 00:04:15,481
that would be a start
in the right direction.

44
00:04:15,655 --> 00:04:18,625
But just because there's no body

45
00:04:18,792 --> 00:04:20,727
doesn't mean somebody
won't be convicted,

46
00:04:20,894 --> 00:04:22,692
because there have
been cases like that.

47
00:04:22,863 --> 00:04:24,263
Well, what if you have a motive?

48
00:04:24,431 --> 00:04:28,300
I mean, you really hate somebody
and you want to kill them?

49
00:04:28,468 --> 00:04:30,369
- How do you do it?
- Yeah, how do you do it?

50
00:04:30,537 --> 00:04:31,835
How do you do it?

51
00:04:32,005 --> 00:04:34,304
Um... Is this a
personal question?

52
00:04:35,675 --> 00:04:41,876
Well, I guess... I guess
most killers make a mistake.

53
00:04:42,048 --> 00:04:47,885
But I suppose if, theoretically,
if you wanted to kill somebody,

54
00:04:48,054 --> 00:04:49,750
you'd first have
to have a motive.

55
00:04:49,923 --> 00:04:51,949
So you'd want to do it.

56
00:04:52,125 --> 00:04:55,823
Then you'd find
somebody to kill.

57
00:04:55,996 --> 00:04:57,555
And you'd have to
get rid of the body.

58
00:04:57,731 --> 00:05:03,170
Yeah. You'd get rid of the body
and then try to frame somebody else.

59
00:05:03,336 --> 00:05:05,498
But the person you frame
has got to have the motive

60
00:05:05,672 --> 00:05:08,164
and the means and the opportunity
to commit the crime, right?

61
00:05:08,341 --> 00:05:09,570
Yeah. Of course.

62
00:05:09,743 --> 00:05:12,054
Well, have you ever heard
of anyone that made a knife

63
00:05:12,078 --> 00:05:14,604
out of ice and then
melted the murder weapon?

64
00:05:14,781 --> 00:05:16,477
No.

65
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:19,313
So either no one's ever done it

66
00:05:19,486 --> 00:05:23,287
or it's such a good trick
no one's ever gotten caught.

67
00:05:23,456 --> 00:05:24,947
Spoken like a true lawyer.

68
00:05:31,865 --> 00:05:34,562
Ben Matlock, waste of time.

69
00:05:34,734 --> 00:05:35,894
Seems to work for him though.

70
00:05:36,036 --> 00:05:37,334
Let's try to get a beer.

71
00:05:37,504 --> 00:05:40,030
You buy, you're the talker.

72
00:05:41,842 --> 00:05:45,006
It's crowded. Maybe
there's a table in the back.

73
00:05:51,985 --> 00:05:53,715
Hey, Carol. Let's go have lunch.

74
00:05:53,887 --> 00:05:55,082
Thanks, Eddie. I can't.

75
00:05:55,255 --> 00:05:57,622
Oh, you can't? What,
he won't let you?

76
00:05:57,791 --> 00:06:00,659
- I'm having lunch with Mark.
- Okay. We'll have dinner then.

77
00:06:00,827 --> 00:06:02,921
- Eight o'clock.
- Eddie, I told you,

78
00:06:03,096 --> 00:06:05,463
it's over. That's it.

79
00:06:05,632 --> 00:06:09,000
It's over? We're over?
After six months? No.

80
00:06:09,169 --> 00:06:12,003
We had a fight.
Who doesn't fight?

81
00:06:12,172 --> 00:06:13,231
But it's not over.

82
00:06:13,406 --> 00:06:16,366
Hey, you don't seem to get it, do you?
She doesn't want to see you anymore.

83
00:06:16,509 --> 00:06:18,034
Why don't you just
get out of her way?

84
00:06:18,211 --> 00:06:20,646
Hey, look, guys, it talks.

85
00:06:20,814 --> 00:06:22,783
Ooh. MAN 2: Ha, ha.

86
00:06:22,949 --> 00:06:24,918
You've got a really big mouth.

87
00:06:25,085 --> 00:06:28,544
- You're beginning to annoy me.
- I said move it.

88
00:06:35,328 --> 00:06:37,661
- See you around.
- Mark.

89
00:06:37,831 --> 00:06:40,630
Leave me alone.
Just leave me alone.

90
00:07:29,416 --> 00:07:31,146
What the hell is going on?

91
00:07:34,354 --> 00:07:35,982
Hey, Eddie.

92
00:07:37,023 --> 00:07:39,822
I thought we were
going to the lake early.

93
00:07:40,961 --> 00:07:43,123
Hey, Eddie? Where are you?

94
00:07:50,804 --> 00:07:52,466
Eddie?

95
00:07:56,276 --> 00:08:00,737
Eddie? Eddie?

96
00:08:13,026 --> 00:08:18,431
Well, the papers are to be on Locke,
Hume, any of the English philosophers.

97
00:08:24,871 --> 00:08:26,191
You wanted to talk to me?

98
00:08:26,272 --> 00:08:30,004
- Yes. Want to sit down?
- Oh, yeah.

99
00:08:35,915 --> 00:08:38,316
Since you last spoke
to the Law Club,

100
00:08:38,485 --> 00:08:41,785
one of our students by
the name of Eddie Price

101
00:08:41,988 --> 00:08:43,752
- has disappeared.
- Yeah?

102
00:08:43,923 --> 00:08:48,623
Yes. Eddie Price's roommate
found blood on the floor of the garage

103
00:08:48,795 --> 00:08:51,264
and a broken beer bottle nearby.

104
00:08:51,431 --> 00:08:54,833
You think...? Do you
think he was killed?

105
00:08:55,702 --> 00:08:58,866
I don't know. He's gone.

106
00:08:59,039 --> 00:09:02,237
Police have been searching for him
for five days. They haven't found him.

107
00:09:02,409 --> 00:09:03,433
Huh.

108
00:09:03,610 --> 00:09:05,511
Maybe he just took off.

109
00:09:05,678 --> 00:09:10,514
Yes, but he left behind his glasses
and an uncashed scholarship check.

110
00:09:10,683 --> 00:09:15,053
Without a body,
murder is hard to prove.

111
00:09:16,156 --> 00:09:17,954
So I've been told.

112
00:09:18,391 --> 00:09:20,656
Police were here and we talked.

113
00:09:21,528 --> 00:09:24,362
Ben, I think you
had a hand in this.

114
00:09:26,032 --> 00:09:27,625
What did I do?

115
00:09:27,801 --> 00:09:30,771
Well, from what I hear, heh,

116
00:09:30,937 --> 00:09:36,467
you all but gave your class a blueprint
of how to commit a perfect murder.

117
00:09:36,643 --> 00:09:40,478
Wait a minute. I'm
talking to these freshmen

118
00:09:40,647 --> 00:09:44,914
on criminal law. We talk
about murder, crimes of all kind.

119
00:09:45,085 --> 00:09:47,605
Yes, but if one of your students
took what you said and went out

120
00:09:47,754 --> 00:09:49,834
- and killed him...
- That's garbage and you know it.

121
00:09:49,956 --> 00:09:51,185
Now, just take it easy.

122
00:09:51,357 --> 00:09:53,292
You teach Physics.

123
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:55,520
Your students don't
build nuclear bombs.

124
00:09:55,695 --> 00:09:57,994
- Well, this is different.
- It's not different.

125
00:09:58,164 --> 00:10:01,965
Look, these are young,
impressionable boys and girls.

126
00:10:02,135 --> 00:10:05,765
Well, I'm just talking
to these kids as a favor.

127
00:10:08,007 --> 00:10:10,203
The police think I'm in on this?

128
00:10:10,376 --> 00:10:12,743
Well, now, I didn't say
you did it intentionally.

129
00:10:12,912 --> 00:10:15,507
I'm saying I
didn't do it at all.

130
00:10:30,597 --> 00:10:32,361
Doggone.

131
00:10:35,368 --> 00:10:37,701
Doggone.

132
00:10:53,853 --> 00:10:59,417
<i>Look down, look down
That lonesome road</i>

133
00:10:59,592 --> 00:11:03,029
<i>Before you travel on</i>

134
00:11:04,097 --> 00:11:08,998
<i>Look up, look up See your maker</i>

135
00:11:09,435 --> 00:11:12,837
<i>Before Gabriel blows his horn</i>

136
00:11:14,507 --> 00:11:16,169
<i>Weary...</i>

137
00:11:17,710 --> 00:11:19,645
Doggone.

138
00:11:20,413 --> 00:11:22,439
You sound sad.

139
00:11:23,283 --> 00:11:24,808
Oh, Leanne,

140
00:11:24,984 --> 00:11:30,446
I try to do a public service and I
wind up sticking my foot right in it.

141
00:11:30,623 --> 00:11:34,583
- What in the world did you do?
- Well,

142
00:11:35,061 --> 00:11:38,225
I'm talking to these
freshmen over at the college.

143
00:11:38,398 --> 00:11:40,594
- You know, Law Club?
- Yeah.

144
00:11:40,767 --> 00:11:43,293
And I'm talking to
them about criminal law.

145
00:11:43,469 --> 00:11:44,528
Yeah?

146
00:11:44,704 --> 00:11:48,505
And one of them asks me if
I've ever seen a perfect murder.

147
00:11:48,675 --> 00:11:51,577
- Yeah?
- And I told him that I had.

148
00:11:53,446 --> 00:11:56,507
And one of them, a fellow
named Eddie-something,

149
00:11:56,683 --> 00:12:01,246
nobody said, but I get the
feeling people didn't like him.

150
00:12:01,421 --> 00:12:03,720
- Uh-huh.
- Kind of a jerk.

151
00:12:03,890 --> 00:12:08,191
- Right.
- Well, anyway, he turned up missing

152
00:12:08,361 --> 00:12:12,355
and they found some blood and
a broken beer bottle in his garage.

153
00:12:12,532 --> 00:12:14,057
Do they think he was killed?

154
00:12:14,234 --> 00:12:15,634
Well, they don't know.

155
00:12:15,802 --> 00:12:20,433
They didn't find a body and
he's been gone for five days.

156
00:12:20,607 --> 00:12:22,667
Oh, the perfect murder, no body.

157
00:12:22,842 --> 00:12:24,708
Yeah.

158
00:12:25,178 --> 00:12:28,342
Dean Rutledge says that he
thinks that maybe somebody did it

159
00:12:28,514 --> 00:12:30,142
because of what I said.

160
00:12:30,316 --> 00:12:33,047
And he said the police think
maybe that happened too.

161
00:12:33,219 --> 00:12:36,280
Oh, Dad. I'm so sorry.

162
00:12:36,456 --> 00:12:40,120
Why couldn't I have just said, "Why
don't we talk about something else?"

163
00:12:40,293 --> 00:12:43,855
Or, "Let's sing 'The Sweetheart
of Sigma Chi,"" or something?

164
00:12:44,364 --> 00:12:46,526
How dare they blame this on you?

165
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:50,495
I mean, here you are
giving your valuable time

166
00:12:50,670 --> 00:12:53,265
trying to teach a bunch
of freshmen about law.

167
00:12:53,439 --> 00:12:55,806
One of them asks you a
question... I mean, Dad,

168
00:12:55,975 --> 00:12:58,342
I know you. You're not
gonna tell them to go fishing,

169
00:12:58,511 --> 00:13:00,480
you're gonna
answer their question.

170
00:13:00,647 --> 00:13:02,775
- Well...
- I mean, you are a citizen

171
00:13:02,949 --> 00:13:05,612
performing a public service.

172
00:13:05,785 --> 00:13:09,119
One of them disappears, they
don't know if he's murdered or not,

173
00:13:09,289 --> 00:13:11,383
and they blame this on you?

174
00:13:12,558 --> 00:13:14,720
- Yeah.
- It's not right.

175
00:13:14,894 --> 00:13:16,920
- No.
- It is just not right.

176
00:13:17,096 --> 00:13:18,325
No, it's not.

177
00:13:18,498 --> 00:13:20,160
When do you see
these kids again?

178
00:13:20,333 --> 00:13:21,733
- Tomorrow, I'm supposed to.
- Yeah?

179
00:13:21,901 --> 00:13:24,393
- Yeah.
- I'm going with you.

180
00:13:36,382 --> 00:13:40,649
I'm Leanne Mclntyre.
And that's my dad.

181
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:42,288
As some of you may know,

182
00:13:42,455 --> 00:13:45,914
his integrity and his
judgment are being challenged.

183
00:13:46,326 --> 00:13:50,195
That is, one of your fellow
classmates, Eddie Price, is missing.

184
00:13:50,363 --> 00:13:51,991
Some people say that happened

185
00:13:52,165 --> 00:13:53,845
because of something
my father said to you.

186
00:13:55,368 --> 00:13:57,269
Now, we've got
several possibilities.

187
00:13:57,437 --> 00:14:01,067
Mr. Price could have gone
off somewhere. We'll find out.

188
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:05,041
Or this could be a
practical joke. We'll find out.

189
00:14:05,845 --> 00:14:07,473
By the way, if this is a joke,

190
00:14:07,647 --> 00:14:09,377
whoever pulled it will
be out of this school

191
00:14:09,549 --> 00:14:11,142
faster than you can
say, "Dave Justice."

192
00:14:13,319 --> 00:14:18,257
And last, but certainly not
least, Mr. Price could be dead.

193
00:14:18,858 --> 00:14:20,884
Maybe someone killed him.

194
00:14:22,095 --> 00:14:23,358
Maybe one of you.

195
00:14:24,997 --> 00:14:26,761
We'll find out.

196
00:14:26,933 --> 00:14:29,596
All of you are members
of this Law Club.

197
00:14:29,769 --> 00:14:32,967
I assume that means you want
to learn something about law.

198
00:14:34,173 --> 00:14:36,608
Well, you're going to because
you're going to help us.

199
00:14:36,776 --> 00:14:39,712
When I say, "We'll find out,"

200
00:14:39,879 --> 00:14:41,905
I mean all of us.

201
00:14:42,148 --> 00:14:46,677
Me, my dad and you.

202
00:14:48,087 --> 00:14:49,988
Could you hold your
question for just a second?

203
00:14:51,624 --> 00:14:54,025
Here's what we need you to do.

204
00:14:54,927 --> 00:14:56,127
You're going to do what we do.

205
00:14:56,295 --> 00:14:59,959
You're going to ask questions
and get answers from anybody,

206
00:15:00,133 --> 00:15:02,762
from each other, and
you're gonna start today.

207
00:15:03,302 --> 00:15:05,430
Now, you had a question? - Yeah.

208
00:15:05,605 --> 00:15:07,870
If Eddie is dead,

209
00:15:08,441 --> 00:15:11,707
- when was he killed?
- As near as the police can tell,

210
00:15:11,878 --> 00:15:13,642
around 8:00 on Tuesday night.

211
00:15:13,813 --> 00:15:17,807
You said that your father's integrity
and judgment are being challenged.

212
00:15:17,984 --> 00:15:21,887
It's been suggested that
something my father said to you

213
00:15:22,054 --> 00:15:23,818
in regards to perfect murders

214
00:15:23,990 --> 00:15:26,585
prompted whatever
happened to Eddie Price.

215
00:15:26,759 --> 00:15:28,421
I don't believe that.

216
00:15:29,962 --> 00:15:31,760
Neither do I.

217
00:15:33,332 --> 00:15:35,961
But you think there's
some truth to that.

218
00:15:36,769 --> 00:15:38,169
I don't know.

219
00:15:38,871 --> 00:15:44,742
I get the feeling from your daughter's
speech that we're all suspects.

220
00:15:44,911 --> 00:15:47,005
Oh, you betcha.

221
00:15:50,450 --> 00:15:53,887
Well, that's it for today. We'll meet
tomorrow morning at 10:00 a.m.

222
00:15:54,053 --> 00:15:58,388
at 1015 Willow Springs Road.
I suggest you write this down.

223
00:15:58,558 --> 00:16:01,187
That's my dad's
house and our office.

224
00:16:01,861 --> 00:16:05,298
Oh, and for your information,
we've been in touch with the police

225
00:16:05,465 --> 00:16:07,730
and they know all about this.

226
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:12,235
So if you don't show up
or decide not to cooperate,

227
00:16:12,405 --> 00:16:14,874
that makes you all
the more suspect.

228
00:16:15,475 --> 00:16:17,171
See you tomorrow.

229
00:16:32,225 --> 00:16:33,784
Thanks.

230
00:16:35,294 --> 00:16:36,762
Well,

231
00:16:37,129 --> 00:16:41,863
kids... Kids as investigators,
that's a first for us.

232
00:16:42,034 --> 00:16:44,629
- Well, they are freshmen in college.
- Yeah.

233
00:16:45,771 --> 00:16:48,969
Makes it that much easier
to keep an eye on them.

234
00:17:18,738 --> 00:17:20,832
Hi. I'm Leanne Mclntyre.

235
00:17:22,208 --> 00:17:23,733
Hi.

236
00:17:23,910 --> 00:17:25,879
I'm looking into the
disappearance of Eddie Price.

237
00:17:26,045 --> 00:17:27,570
I spoke to you this morning.

238
00:17:27,747 --> 00:17:29,613
Yes, ma'am.

239
00:17:38,491 --> 00:17:41,791
- You play?
- Yeah.

240
00:17:43,596 --> 00:17:46,191
So you and Eddie
went to school together?

241
00:17:46,699 --> 00:17:48,031
Yeah.

242
00:17:50,002 --> 00:17:51,470
You two were good friends?

243
00:17:52,772 --> 00:17:54,297
Uh-huh.

244
00:17:54,473 --> 00:17:57,136
You ever answer a question
with more than one word?

245
00:17:58,945 --> 00:18:00,004
Sure.

246
00:18:00,179 --> 00:18:03,149
Good. Here's a little test.

247
00:18:03,883 --> 00:18:06,546
How long did you and
Eddie live together?

248
00:18:06,719 --> 00:18:08,187
Two.

249
00:18:10,089 --> 00:18:12,251
- Years.
- Very good.

250
00:18:12,425 --> 00:18:15,589
I understand Eddie
wasn't a very nice guy.

251
00:18:15,761 --> 00:18:16,922
Depends.

252
00:18:17,096 --> 00:18:19,588
- On what?
- Who you ask.

253
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:24,430
Well, let's just say I was gonna
ask somebody that didn't like him.

254
00:18:24,604 --> 00:18:26,732
What would they say?

255
00:18:28,474 --> 00:18:31,000
- I don't like him.
- Why?

256
00:18:32,678 --> 00:18:34,647
He liked to chase
other guys' girls.

257
00:18:34,814 --> 00:18:36,612
That bother you?

258
00:18:36,782 --> 00:18:38,648
- Why?
- Why what?

259
00:18:39,552 --> 00:18:40,645
Why would it bother me?

260
00:18:40,820 --> 00:18:42,413
Did he ever go after your girl?

261
00:18:43,389 --> 00:18:44,413
No.

262
00:18:44,590 --> 00:18:46,821
Was there ever anyone you knew

263
00:18:46,993 --> 00:18:50,054
who went crazy when
Eddie stole their girl?

264
00:18:50,663 --> 00:18:52,757
- Yeah.
- Who?

265
00:18:53,466 --> 00:18:54,661
Bobby.

266
00:18:56,769 --> 00:18:58,829
- Simpson.
- Bobby Simpson.

267
00:19:00,106 --> 00:19:03,440
What girl did Eddie steal
from Bobby Simpson?

268
00:19:04,176 --> 00:19:05,769
Carol Thompson.

269
00:19:06,479 --> 00:19:10,644
When Eddie stole Carol from Bobby,
what did he do? Did he go what?

270
00:19:10,983 --> 00:19:12,042
Hm.

271
00:19:12,218 --> 00:19:14,881
- Ballistic.
- Ballistic. Great.

272
00:19:15,054 --> 00:19:17,649
It's been wonderful
talking to you.

273
00:19:17,823 --> 00:19:19,951
I may want to talk to you again.

274
00:19:20,760 --> 00:19:22,160
Sure.

275
00:19:27,433 --> 00:19:31,336
Well, thank you anyway. Let me
know if you hear anything, please.

276
00:19:31,504 --> 00:19:33,564
Yeah. Okay.

277
00:19:48,921 --> 00:19:50,287
What are you doing here?

278
00:19:50,456 --> 00:19:52,891
Taking a look at the scene
of the crime. What about you?

279
00:19:53,059 --> 00:19:58,760
Yeah. I figured that whoever
killed Eddie, if he's even dead,

280
00:19:58,931 --> 00:20:01,162
had to kill him in the
garage and move the body.

281
00:20:01,333 --> 00:20:03,773
And I don't think anybody carried
a dead body down the street,

282
00:20:03,903 --> 00:20:07,067
so I figured that
there had to be a car.

283
00:20:07,239 --> 00:20:09,037
Very good, Sherlock.

284
00:20:09,208 --> 00:20:10,904
Sherlock?

285
00:20:11,077 --> 00:20:12,602
- Got any leads?
- No.

286
00:20:12,778 --> 00:20:15,213
No, I was just going
around house to house,

287
00:20:15,381 --> 00:20:17,373
asking if anybody saw anything.

288
00:20:17,550 --> 00:20:18,950
You know, Mark,

289
00:20:19,118 --> 00:20:21,986
everyone saw Eddie dump
that beer over your head.

290
00:20:23,489 --> 00:20:27,290
I know that. I know. Half the Law
Club was there when that happened.

291
00:20:27,460 --> 00:20:30,624
- Police talk to you?
- Yeah.

292
00:20:31,797 --> 00:20:34,289
I guess you got a good
reason to be nervous.

293
00:20:35,634 --> 00:20:39,127
I'm not nervous, Don.

294
00:20:39,305 --> 00:20:42,935
Look, I'm doing this because I
was told to and because I want to.

295
00:21:03,462 --> 00:21:05,829
Hi. CAROL: Hi.

296
00:21:07,032 --> 00:21:09,365
I'd love to play football.

297
00:21:09,835 --> 00:21:12,896
No, they don't let
girls in their game.

298
00:21:13,973 --> 00:21:15,650
We ought to do
something about that.

299
00:21:15,674 --> 00:21:16,835
Yeah.

300
00:21:17,009 --> 00:21:19,274
But you're not here
to talk about football.

301
00:21:19,445 --> 00:21:23,177
No. I want to know a little
bit more about Eddie Price.

302
00:21:24,483 --> 00:21:27,453
He wasn't the kind of boy
you'd take home to Mother.

303
00:21:27,620 --> 00:21:28,986
My parents hated him.

304
00:21:29,188 --> 00:21:32,056
- Why is that?
- I don't know. He was wild.

305
00:21:33,359 --> 00:21:34,827
Unpredictable.

306
00:21:34,994 --> 00:21:37,793
- And you liked that?
- I guess.

307
00:21:37,963 --> 00:21:40,865
He wanted what he
wanted when he wanted it.

308
00:21:42,067 --> 00:21:44,036
Sounds like trouble to me.

309
00:21:44,203 --> 00:21:46,798
Wasn't there some guy you
knew you shouldn't be with,

310
00:21:46,972 --> 00:21:49,601
but you just kept going back?

311
00:21:50,943 --> 00:21:53,037
Yeah, I guess I've
known a couple of those.

312
00:21:53,212 --> 00:21:54,510
Heh.

313
00:21:55,347 --> 00:21:56,906
Every girl should.

314
00:21:59,118 --> 00:22:01,451
So how did Bobby feel when
you dumped him for Eddie?

315
00:22:03,022 --> 00:22:04,752
He didn't like it.

316
00:22:07,593 --> 00:22:09,585
Why are you looking at me?

317
00:22:10,029 --> 00:22:11,292
Just wondering.

318
00:22:12,665 --> 00:22:14,827
Why would I want to kill Eddie?

319
00:22:16,268 --> 00:22:17,964
I didn't do it.

320
00:22:18,938 --> 00:22:22,170
I was at the library
studying from 6 to 11.

321
00:22:22,341 --> 00:22:25,004
And if you don't believe me,
you can ask Debbie Glazer.

322
00:22:25,177 --> 00:22:26,941
We were together.

323
00:22:35,788 --> 00:22:37,347
He poured beer over my head.

324
00:22:37,523 --> 00:22:40,357
I might have slashed
his tires or keyed his car.

325
00:22:40,559 --> 00:22:42,585
He humiliated you
in front of Carol.

326
00:22:42,761 --> 00:22:43,785
I didn't kill him.

327
00:22:43,963 --> 00:22:47,229
What, would you kill somebody
for pouring beer over your head?

328
00:22:47,399 --> 00:22:49,197
Where were you
the night he died?

329
00:22:49,368 --> 00:22:50,802
I was at home, working out.

330
00:22:51,003 --> 00:22:53,165
- Alone?
- Yes, alone.

331
00:22:53,339 --> 00:22:54,637
No alibi.

332
00:22:54,807 --> 00:22:57,106
Hey, I saw you
at the cafe, Bobby.

333
00:22:57,276 --> 00:22:59,336
For all I know, you might
be trying to frame me.

334
00:22:59,511 --> 00:23:01,980
Bobby, I spoke to Buddy
Hart, Eddie's roommate.

335
00:23:02,147 --> 00:23:04,981
He said you hated Eddie
because he took Carol from you.

336
00:23:05,184 --> 00:23:07,949
Hated? Isn't that
a little overstated?

337
00:23:08,120 --> 00:23:10,214
Bobby, you said
you wanted to kill him.

338
00:23:10,389 --> 00:23:13,223
Time out here. Okay, I was madder
than hell when Carol first dumped me

339
00:23:13,392 --> 00:23:15,088
but that was a long time ago.

340
00:23:15,261 --> 00:23:18,060
You may not believe this,
honey, but guys do get over you.

341
00:23:18,631 --> 00:23:20,259
Huh. Uh...

342
00:23:21,033 --> 00:23:24,697
Say Eddie's dead, if he is
dead, would you be glad?

343
00:23:24,870 --> 00:23:26,998
Know what? I don't like
being treated like a suspect.

344
00:23:27,172 --> 00:23:30,142
Don't you get it?
We're all suspects.

345
00:23:30,309 --> 00:23:34,041
Okay, at the time Eddie was
killed, I was home studying.

346
00:23:34,213 --> 00:23:36,273
- Alone?
- Alone, yes.

347
00:23:36,448 --> 00:23:38,383
And I don't have
an alibi either.

348
00:23:38,550 --> 00:23:40,041
You know what? I'm out of here.

349
00:23:40,219 --> 00:23:43,280
You guys wanna play their
game, that's your problem.

350
00:23:43,455 --> 00:23:45,117
Hey, Bobby.

351
00:23:46,692 --> 00:23:48,490
I can prove he was home.

352
00:23:48,661 --> 00:23:50,653
We live in the same
apartment complex.

353
00:23:51,230 --> 00:23:53,358
My parking space is
right across from Bobby's

354
00:23:53,532 --> 00:23:57,936
and on the night we're talking about,
I went in and out a couple of times

355
00:23:58,103 --> 00:23:59,765
and his car was always there.

356
00:23:59,939 --> 00:24:01,965
- You sure?
- I'm positive.

357
00:24:02,141 --> 00:24:06,340
There were a lot of cars. How
can you be sure Bobby's was there?

358
00:24:06,512 --> 00:24:12,042
Well, it's a '67 Mustang
convertible, cherry.

359
00:24:13,152 --> 00:24:15,348
Does that mean it's in
good shape, or the color?

360
00:24:15,521 --> 00:24:16,921
Heh, heh. Both.

361
00:24:18,657 --> 00:24:20,785
Okay, I think we'd
better stop for today.

362
00:24:20,960 --> 00:24:22,121
Okay.

363
00:24:24,863 --> 00:24:26,832
Wait, wait, wait.

364
00:24:27,933 --> 00:24:32,769
I need to tell you before you find
out. Eddie kind of stole one of my girls.

365
00:24:32,938 --> 00:24:34,736
- Yeah, well...
- Who?

366
00:24:34,907 --> 00:24:36,341
Patty.

367
00:24:36,508 --> 00:24:38,636
You went out with Patty? - Yeah.

368
00:24:38,811 --> 00:24:40,575
I was gonna break up with her

369
00:24:40,746 --> 00:24:43,614
and then Eddie stepped
in, and she went with him.

370
00:24:43,782 --> 00:24:45,546
- Were you upset?
- No.

371
00:24:46,218 --> 00:24:49,188
And I didn't kill
him, if he's dead.

372
00:24:49,355 --> 00:24:51,051
Well, where were you that night?

373
00:24:51,223 --> 00:24:53,749
Carol, you know
I was in the play.

374
00:24:53,926 --> 00:24:58,660
I was in the Theater Department's
production of a musical called <i>The Ball.</i>

375
00:24:58,831 --> 00:24:59,958
Still am.

376
00:25:00,132 --> 00:25:02,761
- Oh, you act?
- And sing.

377
00:25:02,935 --> 00:25:04,028
Ah. MATLOCK: Oh.

378
00:25:04,303 --> 00:25:05,965
Well, thank you, Don, for that.

379
00:25:06,138 --> 00:25:08,937
And as Leanne said,
tomorrow, hot and heavy.

380
00:25:09,108 --> 00:25:10,542
- Okay. See you later.
- Bye.

381
00:25:10,709 --> 00:25:12,473
- See you.
- Bye.

382
00:25:17,082 --> 00:25:22,111
- What do you think?
- Oh, I'd love to see Don act and sing.

383
00:25:26,458 --> 00:25:28,538
<i>It's 30 minutes to
curtain, everybody.</i>

384
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:30,288
- Excuse me.
- Yeah?

385
00:25:30,462 --> 00:25:31,486
Could you tell me

386
00:25:31,663 --> 00:25:34,633
if there was a production of
the play last Thursday night?

387
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:38,166
Yeah, Thursday through
Sunday, four shows a week.

388
00:25:38,337 --> 00:25:41,136
Was Don Hubbard in the
production last Thursday night?

389
00:25:41,306 --> 00:25:43,241
- I guess.
- You guess?

390
00:25:43,409 --> 00:25:46,208
- Is he in the play?
- He's in the chorus.

391
00:25:46,378 --> 00:25:49,871
The chorus? Okay.
Any of them here?

392
00:25:50,149 --> 00:25:52,983
- Yeah, but it's before the show, and...
- I'm Leanne Mclntyre.

393
00:25:53,152 --> 00:25:56,486
I'm investigating the
disappearance of Eddie Price.

394
00:25:56,822 --> 00:25:58,154
Oh.

395
00:25:58,323 --> 00:26:01,225
- Then you haven't seen the show?
- No, I haven't.

396
00:26:01,593 --> 00:26:03,186
Come with me.

397
00:26:06,632 --> 00:26:07,793
There you go.

398
00:26:09,201 --> 00:26:13,696
<i>- The Ball</i> is a masquerade.
- I see.

399
00:26:19,411 --> 00:26:22,745
Hello, I'm looking
for Don Hubbard.

400
00:26:24,316 --> 00:26:26,444
- Leanne. Hi.
- Hi.

401
00:26:26,618 --> 00:26:30,282
Listen, let me take you
out to dinner after the show.

402
00:26:31,723 --> 00:26:33,589
Do you wear that
mask for the whole play?

403
00:26:34,226 --> 00:26:37,424
- How about coffee?
- Don...

404
00:26:38,730 --> 00:26:40,562
You came to find out
if I was here that night.

405
00:26:40,732 --> 00:26:43,600
Yes. Do you wear the
mask for the whole play?

406
00:26:43,769 --> 00:26:44,793
Yes.

407
00:26:44,970 --> 00:26:46,490
Then how can anyone
know you were here?

408
00:26:46,638 --> 00:26:49,665
I mean, even the person standing
next to you wouldn't know it was you.

409
00:26:51,977 --> 00:26:53,878
I guess that's true.

410
00:27:07,593 --> 00:27:10,495
I've never been in a
police station before.

411
00:27:10,662 --> 00:27:12,858
Look, maybe we
should just leave.

412
00:27:13,031 --> 00:27:16,024
No, come on. You wanted to
talk to him, so let's talk to him.

413
00:27:16,201 --> 00:27:17,635
Let's go.

414
00:27:20,672 --> 00:27:21,935
Hi, Steve.

415
00:27:22,107 --> 00:27:24,008
You remember Carol
Thompson and Mark Randall?

416
00:27:24,176 --> 00:27:25,439
- Hi.
- Hello.

417
00:27:25,611 --> 00:27:27,239
Uh, hi.

418
00:27:28,147 --> 00:27:31,140
Look, have you finished questioning
everybody in the Law Club?

419
00:27:31,316 --> 00:27:33,945
- Yes.
- Do you have any suspects?

420
00:27:35,587 --> 00:27:38,557
We were hoping you could bring
us up to date on your investigation.

421
00:27:38,724 --> 00:27:40,784
You pretty well
know what we know.

422
00:27:40,959 --> 00:27:43,986
We made an extensive search for the
body and have come up with nothing.

423
00:27:44,596 --> 00:27:47,327
We pieced together
the broken beer bottle

424
00:27:47,499 --> 00:27:49,491
that was found on
the floor of the garage.

425
00:27:49,668 --> 00:27:53,605
There's no identifiable
prints, so far.

426
00:27:53,772 --> 00:27:55,216
And you've checked
the hospitals?

427
00:27:55,240 --> 00:27:56,680
Florida, Georgia
and South Carolina.

428
00:27:56,842 --> 00:27:58,402
You're gonna continue
the investigation?

429
00:27:58,544 --> 00:28:00,308
- Yeah. CAROL: Why
would you pursue this

430
00:28:00,479 --> 00:28:03,916
- when you don't know if he's dead?
- Eddie has been missing for ten days.

431
00:28:04,082 --> 00:28:06,415
He's without his
wallet, his glasses,

432
00:28:06,585 --> 00:28:09,077
and none of his credit
cards have been used.

433
00:28:09,254 --> 00:28:12,691
I think it's fair to assume
that he was murdered.

434
00:28:12,858 --> 00:28:15,293
But without a body,
you can't go to trial.

435
00:28:15,460 --> 00:28:19,659
That's not true. Murder cases
do go to trial without a body.

436
00:28:19,831 --> 00:28:23,461
In Los Angeles, the
Beverly Hills Boys Club case,

437
00:28:23,635 --> 00:28:25,297
they were convicted. No body.

438
00:28:25,470 --> 00:28:28,702
And in San Diego, against a
police officer, they convicted.

439
00:28:28,874 --> 00:28:29,898
No body.

440
00:28:30,075 --> 00:28:33,045
There's another
possibility. Bodies show up.

441
00:28:33,212 --> 00:28:35,579
Weeks, months
later, they're found.

442
00:28:36,281 --> 00:28:37,749
Who are the suspects?

443
00:28:38,517 --> 00:28:40,197
- You asked that already.
- I know. I know.

444
00:28:40,352 --> 00:28:42,352
We have the right to
know if we'll be investigated.

445
00:28:42,521 --> 00:28:46,083
- You'll be told at the appropriate time.
- Okay. Thank you, Steve.

446
00:28:46,258 --> 00:28:47,851
We should go.

447
00:28:57,703 --> 00:29:00,730
I'm sorry I lost
it the other day.

448
00:29:01,006 --> 00:29:03,635
I could have handled
it better myself.

449
00:29:04,343 --> 00:29:07,177
So I hear you've started
a little investigation?

450
00:29:07,346 --> 00:29:09,941
Yeah. I've got
to look into this.

451
00:29:10,115 --> 00:29:11,310
I know.

452
00:29:11,483 --> 00:29:14,612
The reason I called you is
because I remembered something

453
00:29:14,786 --> 00:29:16,755
that I should have told you.

454
00:29:16,955 --> 00:29:19,220
- How about a hot dog?
- Well...

455
00:29:19,391 --> 00:29:20,450
Two, all the way.

456
00:29:20,626 --> 00:29:23,186
Did you ever hear of the
Charles Lipper Scholarship?

457
00:29:23,895 --> 00:29:24,988
No.

458
00:29:25,163 --> 00:29:29,191
At the end of the first semester we
give it to the most deserving freshman.

459
00:29:29,368 --> 00:29:33,567
Now, Mark Randall was one
of the two finalists for the award.

460
00:29:33,739 --> 00:29:35,264
- Did he get it?
- No.

461
00:29:35,440 --> 00:29:36,635
Eddie Price did.

462
00:29:36,808 --> 00:29:40,142
But I was the one that had
to tell Mark that he lost out.

463
00:29:40,312 --> 00:29:43,646
- How did he take it?
- Badly. He was very upset.

464
00:29:43,815 --> 00:29:49,721
You see, Mark figures that
Eddie had sabotaged his chances

465
00:29:49,888 --> 00:29:52,619
by accusing him of cheating.

466
00:29:52,791 --> 00:29:55,420
Sounds like something
Eddie would do.

467
00:29:55,594 --> 00:29:58,962
Maybe. But it had nothing
to do with our decision.

468
00:29:59,131 --> 00:30:03,296
Now, I tried explaining this to
Mark, but he just wouldn't believe me.

469
00:30:03,468 --> 00:30:05,164
He became very angry.

470
00:30:05,337 --> 00:30:07,067
How angry?

471
00:30:07,506 --> 00:30:11,841
Very. And then later, I heard
there was some kind of fight.

472
00:30:17,249 --> 00:30:20,276
Why didn't you tell us
you had a fight with Eddie?

473
00:30:20,686 --> 00:30:23,451
Because it wasn't a
real fight. I pushed him.

474
00:30:23,622 --> 00:30:26,057
He poured beer over my head.

475
00:30:27,225 --> 00:30:29,057
- I didn't kill him.
- Mark...

476
00:30:29,227 --> 00:30:32,789
No, no, no. Wait, you're putting
together a circumstantial case against me

477
00:30:32,964 --> 00:30:35,866
just because I can't prove where I
was the night that Eddie disappeared?

478
00:30:36,034 --> 00:30:39,334
- Well, neither can Bobby.
- Yes, but we know where his car was.

479
00:30:40,005 --> 00:30:42,605
And Don says that he was in the
play, but who knows that for sure?

480
00:30:42,741 --> 00:30:45,074
The whole chorus wears masks.

481
00:30:46,144 --> 00:30:50,138
Carol, I did what you suggested
and I called your friend Debbie Glazer.

482
00:30:50,615 --> 00:30:52,743
And she told you we
were studying together.

483
00:30:52,918 --> 00:30:56,150
Yes, but she remembers
something you don't.

484
00:30:56,421 --> 00:30:57,480
Well, what?

485
00:30:57,656 --> 00:31:00,626
When you and Debbie came out
of the library that night at 11:00,

486
00:31:00,792 --> 00:31:04,229
your car wasn't in the spot
where you said you'd parked it.

487
00:31:04,996 --> 00:31:08,763
I forgot where I parked my
car. I didn't think it was important.

488
00:31:08,934 --> 00:31:11,563
Was another car parked
where you thought yours was?

489
00:31:11,737 --> 00:31:13,865
Well, yeah. I guess.

490
00:31:14,039 --> 00:31:16,372
- Yes or no?
- Yes.

491
00:31:16,541 --> 00:31:19,602
Carol, I mean, I'm not an
overpriced criminal lawyer,

492
00:31:19,778 --> 00:31:21,838
but I doubt you
misplaced your car.

493
00:31:22,013 --> 00:31:24,448
I think it was in the wrong
spot for another reason.

494
00:31:24,616 --> 00:31:27,484
- What reason?
- You moved it.

495
00:31:27,652 --> 00:31:31,248
Are you crazy? I was
in the library studying.

496
00:31:31,823 --> 00:31:33,985
Obviously only part of the time.

497
00:31:34,159 --> 00:31:36,390
I think you left the library.
You went somewhere,

498
00:31:36,561 --> 00:31:39,360
you came back and found
that someone was in your spot,

499
00:31:39,531 --> 00:31:41,557
so you had to park elsewhere.

500
00:31:42,501 --> 00:31:44,367
Are you calling me a liar?

501
00:31:45,737 --> 00:31:47,035
How dare you?

502
00:31:47,205 --> 00:31:49,436
Carol, you hated Eddie Price.

503
00:31:49,608 --> 00:31:53,443
I mean, you dumped Bobby for him
and then Eddie treated you like crap.

504
00:31:53,612 --> 00:31:55,240
Would you look at her?

505
00:31:55,414 --> 00:31:58,907
Do you really think that Carol
could have moved Eddie's body?

506
00:31:59,084 --> 00:32:00,780
Can we stop?

507
00:32:00,952 --> 00:32:03,183
I've had all I can
handle for now.

508
00:32:03,355 --> 00:32:04,721
See you.

509
00:32:08,927 --> 00:32:10,395
Go ahead.

510
00:32:23,041 --> 00:32:25,533
- How could you do that to me?
- Do what?

511
00:32:25,710 --> 00:32:27,702
Where do you get off
accusing me of murder?

512
00:32:27,879 --> 00:32:31,247
- Hey, it was just a joke.
- Oh, yeah. It was really funny.

513
00:32:49,067 --> 00:32:51,935
Hey! Hey, Mark!

514
00:32:52,103 --> 00:32:54,265
Hey! Hey!

515
00:32:54,906 --> 00:32:56,135
Hey, Mark! Hey!

516
00:32:56,842 --> 00:32:59,073
What the hell are you doing?

517
00:32:59,611 --> 00:33:01,876
Whoever killed Eddie,
they had to move the body.

518
00:33:02,047 --> 00:33:04,167
There had to be blood, so
I was just looking to see...

519
00:33:04,282 --> 00:33:06,808
Looking in my car? You were
looking for blood in my car?

520
00:33:06,985 --> 00:33:09,625
- You think I killed Eddie Price? Huh?
- Get off of me! Cut it out!

521
00:33:09,788 --> 00:33:13,748
- Hey, what's going on?
- All right, you two! Hey, hey, hey!

522
00:33:13,925 --> 00:33:15,291
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

523
00:33:15,460 --> 00:33:16,792
What's going on?

524
00:33:16,962 --> 00:33:19,830
He saw me at the cafe and he knew
about the fight over the scholarship.

525
00:33:19,998 --> 00:33:22,718
You knew if anything happened
to Eddie, I'd be the likeliest suspect.

526
00:33:22,868 --> 00:33:26,771
That's really creative, Mark. You know,
you could have a real future in fiction.

527
00:33:31,376 --> 00:33:34,938
If you're up for the show,
I'll leave tickets at the door.

528
00:33:45,223 --> 00:33:48,318
Nobody can confirm that Don
was at the theater that night.

529
00:33:48,493 --> 00:33:52,555
- What do you think?
- I don't like that guy. I really don't.

530
00:34:20,625 --> 00:34:22,958
- Hi.
- Hi.

531
00:34:23,128 --> 00:34:26,530
I'm a friend of Don Hubbard's. Could
you tell me where I might find him?

532
00:34:26,698 --> 00:34:28,257
Sure. Around the back.

533
00:34:28,433 --> 00:34:31,494
- Just follow the yellow brick road.
- Thanks.

534
00:34:54,059 --> 00:34:55,789
Hi, Don.

535
00:34:58,129 --> 00:34:59,529
What are you doing here?

536
00:35:00,265 --> 00:35:01,858
Hi.

537
00:35:04,069 --> 00:35:07,665
Wow, you must be Karen Williams.
- Mm-hm.

538
00:35:07,839 --> 00:35:09,501
You're a very lucky guy, Don.

539
00:35:09,674 --> 00:35:13,611
Not only is your friend attractive,
she also pays you $4,000 a month.

540
00:35:13,778 --> 00:35:17,442
- Heh. What are you talking about?
- I went to your bank.

541
00:35:17,616 --> 00:35:20,745
Their friendly computer
shows that Ms. Williams here

542
00:35:20,919 --> 00:35:23,479
makes monthly deposits
into your account.

543
00:35:25,624 --> 00:35:29,720
- What do you do for that money, Don?
- Why don't you leave?

544
00:35:29,894 --> 00:35:33,763
Darling, my honor
doesn't need defending.

545
00:35:33,932 --> 00:35:35,833
See what the lady wants.

546
00:35:36,668 --> 00:35:38,364
What do you want?

547
00:35:38,570 --> 00:35:40,562
I told you where I was
the night Eddie Price died.

548
00:35:40,739 --> 00:35:42,435
But no one can verify that.

549
00:35:42,607 --> 00:35:44,439
- What night was that?
- The 30th.

550
00:35:44,609 --> 00:35:47,408
- I was on stage.
- He was there.

551
00:35:47,579 --> 00:35:54,144
The 30th was my birthday and I went
to the play and then brought him home.

552
00:35:54,319 --> 00:35:56,982
How would you know
that was Don on stage?

553
00:35:57,155 --> 00:35:59,989
Oh, I'd know Don no
matter what he has on.

554
00:36:00,158 --> 00:36:03,993
Besides, you see that
bracelet? He was wearing it.

555
00:36:04,162 --> 00:36:06,563
I gave it to him
for my birthday.

556
00:36:06,731 --> 00:36:08,256
Ah.

557
00:36:08,533 --> 00:36:11,196
Well, happy belated birthday.

558
00:36:30,021 --> 00:36:31,819
Here she comes.

559
00:36:41,766 --> 00:36:43,511
I got here as fast as
I could. What's up?

560
00:36:43,535 --> 00:36:46,130
I wanna look inside your car.

561
00:36:46,571 --> 00:36:49,234
Inside my car? Why
is he searching my car?

562
00:36:49,407 --> 00:36:51,647
He thinks the reason your car
wasn't where you remembered

563
00:36:51,810 --> 00:36:54,974
that night at the library might have
something to do with the murder.

564
00:36:55,914 --> 00:36:58,850
- Hey, Leanne, look at that.
- What's that?

565
00:36:59,751 --> 00:37:02,311
Something shiny
on that gas pedal.

566
00:37:03,021 --> 00:37:05,183
What's that shiny
spot on your gas pedal?

567
00:37:05,356 --> 00:37:08,690
- I don't know.
- Looks like varnish.

568
00:37:08,860 --> 00:37:11,591
How did varnish
get on my gas pedal?

569
00:37:13,765 --> 00:37:16,496
Now, this is not a court of law.

570
00:37:16,668 --> 00:37:19,832
This is just a bunch of us
having a discussion, okay?

571
00:37:20,271 --> 00:37:21,295
Okay.

572
00:37:21,473 --> 00:37:27,003
And so, I'll ask some
questions, and why don't I...?

573
00:37:27,178 --> 00:37:29,613
I'll start with Bobby.

574
00:37:29,781 --> 00:37:31,909
- Fire away.
- Okay, okay.

575
00:37:32,083 --> 00:37:36,316
Now, you and Carol used to date?

576
00:37:36,988 --> 00:37:39,150
Yeah. We went out for a while.

577
00:37:39,324 --> 00:37:41,919
How long is a while?

578
00:37:43,061 --> 00:37:44,859
- A little over a year.
- Oh.

579
00:37:45,029 --> 00:37:47,828
For a fellow your age,
a year is a long time.

580
00:37:47,999 --> 00:37:51,731
You must have
thought quite a lot of her.

581
00:37:51,903 --> 00:37:54,702
I did. I still do.

582
00:37:55,373 --> 00:37:57,501
Who caused the
break-up between you?

583
00:37:57,909 --> 00:37:58,968
She did.

584
00:37:59,144 --> 00:38:03,081
Could you tell us why Carol
wanted to break it off with you?

585
00:38:05,784 --> 00:38:10,984
Carol said that she felt that she was
just too young to handle a commitment.

586
00:38:11,156 --> 00:38:12,488
Oh.

587
00:38:12,824 --> 00:38:17,228
That's not what a young man in
love with a girl wants to hear, is it?

588
00:38:17,395 --> 00:38:19,159
I understood.

589
00:38:19,764 --> 00:38:24,828
You understood that the girl
you loved wanted to play the field?

590
00:38:25,003 --> 00:38:29,941
I understood that she felt that she
was too young to tie herself down.

591
00:38:30,942 --> 00:38:33,309
But she did...

592
00:38:36,147 --> 00:38:38,378
didn't she? The day after
you broke up with her,

593
00:38:38,550 --> 00:38:40,678
she started going
out with Eddie Price.

594
00:38:41,719 --> 00:38:43,415
- Yeah.
- Make you mad?

595
00:38:45,156 --> 00:38:49,252
I told you before, I was angry,
yeah. But that went away.

596
00:38:49,427 --> 00:38:50,827
Yeah.

597
00:38:50,995 --> 00:38:54,557
Yeah. Now, the night
Eddie disappeared,

598
00:38:54,732 --> 00:38:57,566
you were in your room studying?

599
00:38:57,735 --> 00:38:58,759
Yes.

600
00:38:58,937 --> 00:39:03,841
The proof we have of that is that
your car was parked down front.

601
00:39:04,008 --> 00:39:05,704
That's right. It was.

602
00:39:06,411 --> 00:39:09,677
Well, how about
Carol's car that night?

603
00:39:09,848 --> 00:39:12,682
- Did you drive it?
- No.

604
00:39:12,851 --> 00:39:15,616
I didn't. I told you, I
was at home. So...

605
00:39:15,987 --> 00:39:18,422
Uh, ahem...

606
00:39:20,758 --> 00:39:23,193
This is the gas
pedal off Carol's car.

607
00:39:23,361 --> 00:39:25,921
See that shiny spot right there?

608
00:39:26,731 --> 00:39:27,755
Yeah, I see it.

609
00:39:27,932 --> 00:39:31,198
I think that's varnish.
What about you?

610
00:39:31,903 --> 00:39:33,201
- Could be.
- Yeah.

611
00:39:34,005 --> 00:39:35,598
And, uh...

612
00:39:37,175 --> 00:39:40,339
- And this is...
- What are you doing with my shoe?

613
00:39:41,045 --> 00:39:42,377
I borrowed it.

614
00:39:43,581 --> 00:39:44,879
What the hell is going on?

615
00:39:45,049 --> 00:39:47,049
I don't have to sit here
and answer your questions.

616
00:39:47,218 --> 00:39:51,383
- I'm out of here.
- Hey, Bob, you got something to hide?

617
00:39:51,556 --> 00:39:52,888
- No.
- Then sit down.

618
00:39:53,057 --> 00:39:54,923
Answer the man's questions.

619
00:39:55,093 --> 00:39:57,528
Who is he to
interrogate me like this?

620
00:39:57,695 --> 00:40:00,460
Look, Bobby, if you
haven't done anything wrong

621
00:40:00,632 --> 00:40:02,692
then you got nothing
to worry about.

622
00:40:07,305 --> 00:40:08,671
It's up to you.

623
00:40:16,281 --> 00:40:17,715
Now,

624
00:40:19,550 --> 00:40:24,579
a lot of this is supposition.

625
00:40:24,756 --> 00:40:30,218
But I think it would be good
for all of you in Law Club

626
00:40:30,395 --> 00:40:33,263
to see how a lawyer
might put a case together,

627
00:40:33,431 --> 00:40:36,629
if you decide to go
ahead and go into law.

628
00:40:38,002 --> 00:40:43,441
So you see this shiny spot here

629
00:40:43,608 --> 00:40:45,634
on the bottom of
your running shoe?

630
00:40:46,811 --> 00:40:49,337
- I see it.
- I think that's varnish too.

631
00:40:49,514 --> 00:40:54,475
I think it's the same varnish that's
on the gas pedal from Carol's car.

632
00:40:54,652 --> 00:40:57,520
Your uncle owns a
speedboat, doesn't he?

633
00:40:57,689 --> 00:41:02,024
- An old mahogany inboard?
- Yeah, he does.

634
00:41:02,193 --> 00:41:08,064
Are you aware that
on September 30th

635
00:41:08,232 --> 00:41:11,999
at 5:00 in the evening

636
00:41:12,170 --> 00:41:14,901
a fresh coat of varnish
was applied to that boat?

637
00:41:15,506 --> 00:41:17,566
No, I wasn't.

638
00:41:18,076 --> 00:41:23,242
I believe the
varnish on that boat

639
00:41:23,414 --> 00:41:25,713
is the same as the varnish
on your running shoe

640
00:41:25,883 --> 00:41:28,409
and the gas pedal
from Carol's car.

641
00:41:29,287 --> 00:41:30,846
That doesn't prove anything.

642
00:41:31,589 --> 00:41:35,924
No. No. No. Maybe not.

643
00:41:36,594 --> 00:41:38,187
But according to your uncle,

644
00:41:38,363 --> 00:41:42,960
that varnish was applied
between 5 and 6:30 that evening,

645
00:41:43,134 --> 00:41:46,263
and would have been
dry by 1:00 in the morning.

646
00:41:46,437 --> 00:41:53,241
And that means that you might
have stepped on that boat that evening.

647
00:41:55,046 --> 00:41:57,777
Did you lie about staying
home and studying, Bobby?

648
00:41:58,983 --> 00:42:00,144
No.

649
00:42:00,918 --> 00:42:04,082
And you said you didn't
drive Carol's car that evening,

650
00:42:04,255 --> 00:42:08,158
but there's that varnish
on the gas pedal.

651
00:42:08,326 --> 00:42:09,453
Did you lie again?

652
00:42:10,061 --> 00:42:11,586
No.

653
00:42:11,763 --> 00:42:12,787
Well...

654
00:42:13,865 --> 00:42:19,702
Well, I think you
might have killed Eddie.

655
00:42:20,938 --> 00:42:24,500
I think you might have slipped
out of your apartment that night.

656
00:42:24,675 --> 00:42:26,337
You didn't take your car,

657
00:42:26,511 --> 00:42:30,346
because you knew that
somebody might notice it was gone.

658
00:42:30,515 --> 00:42:33,542
I think you might have
used the keys to Carol's car

659
00:42:33,718 --> 00:42:36,381
that she gave you
when you were dating.

660
00:42:36,988 --> 00:42:41,358
I think you might have driven her
car out of the parking lot at the library,

661
00:42:41,526 --> 00:42:48,490
over to Eddie's house, killed him, put
him in the car, driven him to the boat,

662
00:42:49,734 --> 00:42:53,671
taken him out to sea, and
dumped his body overboard.

663
00:42:54,839 --> 00:42:57,399
You're dreaming, Mr. Matlock.

664
00:42:58,709 --> 00:43:03,647
I... As we were discussing
in Law Club that day,

665
00:43:03,815 --> 00:43:06,478
I don't think there is such
a thing as a perfect murder.

666
00:43:06,651 --> 00:43:10,247
I mean, usually something
is overlooked, missed,

667
00:43:10,421 --> 00:43:15,359
or sometimes something
unexpected happens,

668
00:43:15,526 --> 00:43:22,365
like maybe when you drove Carol's
car back to the parking lot at the library

669
00:43:22,533 --> 00:43:26,493
somebody was in her spot, so
you had to park somewhere else.

670
00:43:26,671 --> 00:43:28,970
Maybe she came out and
forgot where she had parked.

671
00:43:29,140 --> 00:43:31,439
Nobody would have
thought anything about it,

672
00:43:31,609 --> 00:43:37,674
except that night a murder
may have been committed,

673
00:43:37,849 --> 00:43:43,948
and so everything, everything, no
matter how small, had a spotlight on it.

674
00:43:46,791 --> 00:43:50,353
Did you drive Carol's car with
Eddie's body to the boat that night?

675
00:43:50,528 --> 00:43:53,828
You can't prove that
I killed Eddie Price.

676
00:43:54,198 --> 00:43:56,497
Mr. Matlock, you can't
prove that Eddie's dead.

677
00:43:56,667 --> 00:43:57,999
No.

678
00:43:59,270 --> 00:44:00,863
No, I can't.

679
00:44:02,173 --> 00:44:03,937
There's no body.

680
00:44:04,442 --> 00:44:05,842
There may never be a body.

681
00:44:06,911 --> 00:44:09,039
I was just making suppositions.

682
00:44:09,213 --> 00:44:12,809
But I tell you, as sure
as we're all sitting here,

683
00:44:13,851 --> 00:44:18,653
if I'm making these
suppositions, the police will too.

684
00:44:24,462 --> 00:44:27,830
Well, I guess that's it,

685
00:44:29,167 --> 00:44:31,864
for me and your Law Club.

686
00:44:32,403 --> 00:44:34,599
I can't say it's an
experience that I've enjoyed.

687
00:44:34,772 --> 00:44:36,866
I'll never forget it.

688
00:44:37,041 --> 00:44:39,135
I'm sure you won't either.

689
00:44:39,343 --> 00:44:43,246
Though I wish you all well.

690
00:44:45,783 --> 00:44:47,445
And goodbye.

691
00:45:14,812 --> 00:45:16,075
So, what do you think?

692
00:45:17,014 --> 00:45:19,210
Well, I don't know.

693
00:45:19,383 --> 00:45:23,218
I try to do something good and I
wind up sticking my foot right in it.

694
00:45:23,387 --> 00:45:24,878
You did something good.

695
00:45:25,423 --> 00:45:28,791
Well, yeah. But if the boy died,

696
00:45:28,960 --> 00:45:31,361
then maybe something I said
did have something to do with it.

697
00:45:31,529 --> 00:45:37,628
Maybe not, but I wish I'd just
taught them how to make cornbread.

698
00:45:38,369 --> 00:45:40,201
You don't make good cornbread.

699
00:45:40,371 --> 00:45:41,703
Mm.

700
00:45:42,240 --> 00:45:44,732
- You know what I mean.
- Yeah. Heh, heh.

701
00:45:45,376 --> 00:45:47,811
Oh, I know you, Dad, and I
know what you're gonna do.

702
00:45:47,979 --> 00:45:50,608
You're gonna moan and
groan, and carry on like this.

703
00:45:50,781 --> 00:45:55,185
And the minute somebody asks you
to teach a class or sweep out a school,

704
00:45:55,353 --> 00:45:56,981
you're gonna say yes.

705
00:45:57,688 --> 00:46:00,715
- Well...
- We've had plenty of talks about this.

706
00:46:00,891 --> 00:46:04,453
It's called giving something back
to your community, remember?

707
00:46:05,162 --> 00:46:08,291
Yeah, but this time I did
stick my foot right in it.

708
00:46:08,466 --> 00:46:09,991
Well, maybe next time you won't.

709
00:46:10,167 --> 00:46:12,693
- Yeah. Maybe not.
- Maybe not.

710
00:46:15,006 --> 00:46:16,736
You wanna go back?

711
00:46:18,042 --> 00:46:19,476
No.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
